UNIT lint_leak
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs, the language-leak lint (leakLint, its quote blanker stripQuoted, and the pieces of cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs it reads each line with); reproduce it exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG lint_leak(text: text, is_primary: bool) -> json
ENVELOPE text is any well-formed text (no unpaired surrogate): any characters, any line breaks, any length; it need not be
  a valid SIR file. is_primary is a bool. A non-text text or a non-bool is_primary is outside the envelope (the package
  fails on a non-text).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION The language-leak lint of SIR v0.5, the two-way firewall of schema section 23b. The text is cut into PHYSICAL
  LINES (R1) and every line is judged ON ITS OWN: no line's verdict depends on another line. For a PRIMARY (is_primary
  true) the line's content pieces (R3, R4) are checked against TABLE DIALECT; citations are never read. For a LANGUAGE FILE
  (is_primary false) a line that opens with a directive name of the primary's register (TABLE REGISTER) is a hit (R6). The
  result is a map with the single key "hits", holding a list of maps with exactly the keys "line" (the 1-based number of
  the physical line, a whole number), "what" (the label of the row that matched, verbatim) and "text" (the DISPLAY of that
  line, R7).
BEHAVIOR
R1 LINES: the text is cut at every CR LF pair, every lone CR and every lone LF; "U+2028" and "U+2029" are not breaks. From
  each line, as cut, every "U+FEFF" of its LEADING RUN (the longest prefix made only of spaces, tabs and "U+FEFF") is
  removed and its spaces and tabs are kept in order, so of "U+FEFF", space, "U+FEFF", "x" both marks go and a space then
  "x" remain; a "U+FEFF" after the first other character stays. Then any trailing run of spaces and tabs is removed.
  Lines are numbered from 1 in this cut, blank lines included. The empty text is one empty line; a final break adds an empty last line. Only spaces
  and tabs are SPACING in this unit: no other whitespace character (no-break space, "U+3000", "U+2028") indents, separates,
  trims or blanks anything, except where R6 names the GLUE set.
R2 Per line: INDENT = its leading run of spaces and tabs; CONTENT = the rest. A line whose CONTENT is empty yields nothing.
  A line is a COMMENT LINE when CONTENT starts with "#"; otherwise CONTENT is split by R3.
R3 TRAILING COMMENT: the first "#" of CONTENT that has a space or tab just before it and a space, a tab or the end of
  CONTENT just after it, and that does not lie inside a QUOTED LITERAL of CONTENT (R5), splits CONTENT: CODE = the text
  before that "#" with its trailing spaces and tabs removed; COMMENT = the text after it with spaces and tabs removed at
  both ends (possibly empty). With no such "#" (a "#" glued to text, after another whitespace character, or inside a
  literal), CODE = CONTENT and there is no COMMENT.
R4 PRIMARY mode (is_primary true). The line's SEGMENTS: for a COMMENT LINE, one segment = CONTENT without its leading run
  of "#". Otherwise: when INDENT is empty, NAME = CODE up to its first space or tab (all of CODE when it has none), less
  one final ":" if it ends with one; when that NAME is a NAMESHAPE (an ASCII capital letter followed by zero or more ASCII
  capitals, ASCII digits or hyphens) other than "CITED", it is grammar, not content, and CODE becomes what follows it (from
  that space or tab on; empty when there is none). This holds for every NAMESHAPE, known or not, except "CITED": a
  column-0 "CITED" (or "CITED:") is a citation keyword, not a name, and CODE is kept whole. Then BODY = the text of CODE,
  read with one space put before it (call it ATTRTEXT), up to its first ATTRIBUTE MARK, spaces and tabs removed at both
  ends; all of it, so trimmed, when it has none. ATTRIBUTE MARK = one or more spaces or tabs, then CITED, EARNED or ANCHOR
  (upper case), then a space, a tab or the end, whose keyword does not lie inside a QUOTED LITERAL of ATTRTEXT (R5): a
  keyword inside a literal is data. What follows a mark is a citation and is never read. So a column-0 "CITED x" has an
  empty BODY (its own keyword is the first mark), while "CITED: x" has no mark there and its BODY is all of CODE. The SEGMENTS are BODY, then the COMMENT if there is one (the COMMENT is
  not cut at marks). Each segment is BLANKED (R5) and every row of TABLE DIALECT whose condition holds on the blanked
  segment yields a hit for this line, labelled with the row's label. Order: segment by segment, rows in table order
  within a segment; a label already hit on this line is not repeated (one hit per line and label: the two "JS API name"
  rows and the two segments share). The hit's text is the DISPLAY of the ORIGINAL line, never of a segment or of the
  blanked form.
R5 QUOTED LITERAL and BLANKING: literals of a text are found left to right and never overlap. A straight double quote
  OPENS a literal only at a TOKEN START: it is the first character of the text being read, or the character just before
  it is one of TABLE OPENERS, or it comes directly after the closing quote of a literal just found (so two adjacent
  literals are two literals). Any other quote (after a letter, a digit, a period, a slash, a backslash, a single quote, a
  backquote, a "#", a no-break space or any character not in TABLE OPENERS) is an ordinary character. An opened literal ends
  at the next straight double quote that is not the second member of an ESCAPE PAIR. An ESCAPE PAIR is a backslash and
  the one CODE POINT after it, whatever it is (a quote, a backslash, "U+2028", "U+2029", a no-break space, a character
  above "U+FFFF"); a backslash that is the last character has nothing to pair with. When an opened literal has no ending
  quote, that quote and every later quote of the text open nothing (no literal is found from there on). The search for
  the next opener resumes just after a literal's closing quote. Only the straight double quote delimits (single quotes and
  backquotes are ordinary). To BLANK a segment, every quoted literal found in that segment (searched afresh, the
  segment's own first character being a token start) is replaced by an empty pair of straight double quotes; all else is
  kept. R3 uses the literals found in CONTENT, R4 those found in ATTRTEXT, by the same rule.
R6 LANGUAGE mode (is_primary false): a line yields one hit labelled "behavior in a language file" when INDENT is empty,
  CONTENT does not start with "#", and its FIRST TOKEN (CONTENT up to its first space or tab, all of it when there is
  none) either (a) is a name listed P in TABLE REGISTER or a RULE NAME ("R" then one or more ASCII digits), or is such a
  name followed by one ":"; or (b) GLUED: its longest leading run of the form NAMESHAPE is a P name or a RULE NAME and is
  followed directly by a code point of the GLUE set, or by one ":" and then a code point of the GLUE set ("R1:" then
  "U+200B" is glued; "R1::" then "U+200B" is not); or (c) INVISIBLE LEAD: CONTENT opens with a run of one or more GLUE
  code points (the LEAD), then the longest NAMESHAPE run N, and N is followed by a space, a tab, a ":", a GLUE code point
  or the end of the line; N is a P name or a RULE NAME. Under (c) nothing after that one following character is read:
  after a lead "R1:x", "R1::" and "R1:" then "U+200B" are hits alike. A line opening with a GLUE code point that does not
  meet (c) yields nothing. Comments, continuation lines, every other name (both-register,
  language-only, unknown, "CITED") and TABLE DIALECT yield nothing. Hits are in line order, at most one per line.
R7 DISPLAY of a line: remove its leading spaces and tabs (no other character), then keep only its first 96 CODE POINTS
  (a character above "U+FFFF" counts as one, as every other character does). The cut never splits a character.
R8 WORD character = an ASCII letter, an ASCII digit or the underscore; no other character is one. In TABLE DIALECT, "not
  preceded by a WORD character" also holds at the start of the segment, "not followed" at its end; STANDALONE = both.
  Every literal in a condition matches case-sensitively unless its row says otherwise.
TABLE DIALECT — condition on the BLANKED segment | label, verbatim
  "/^" occurs, and "$/" occurs after it with no "U+2028" or "U+2029" between them; adjacent counts, as in "/^$/" | "anchored regex literal"
  a backslash, then one of the letters w s d W S D b B, that letter not followed by a WORD character | "regex class escape"
  a backslash, then "p{" | "regex property escape"
  not preceded by a WORD character: "Math." then a WORD character; or "String.prototype", "parseInt", "parseFloat" or "BigInt" not followed by a WORD character; or any of "Number(", "String(", "Boolean(", "BigInt("; or, not preceded by a WORD character OR preceded by ".": any of "toLowerCase(", "toUpperCase(", "charCodeAt(", "codePointAt(", "localeCompare(", "normalize(", "padStart(", "padEnd(" | "JS API name"
  not preceded by a WORD character: one of "JSON", "Object", "Array", "Reflect", "Symbol", "Intl", "Number", "String", "BigInt", "Boolean", "Date", "RegExp", "Promise", then ".", then an ASCII letter of either case | "JS API name"
  not preceded by a WORD character: "fancy-regex", "fancy_regex" or "to_lowercase" not followed by a WORD character; or one of "f64", "u8", "u64", "i64", "usize", "String", "Vec", "char", "str", "Box", "HashMap", "BTreeMap" then "::"; or "Option<" or "Vec<"; anywhere, preceded by anything: "&str" not followed by a WORD character | "Rust API name"
  not preceded by a WORD character: optionally "new", then "line", then one hyphen, one space or nothing, then "terminator"; ASCII letters match in either case and no other letter folds; anything may follow | "line-terminator mechanism"
  "null" or "undefined" STANDALONE | "JS value name (say none)"
TABLE OPENERS — the character just before a quote that may open a literal (R5) | meaning
  " " (space), "U+0009" (tab) | separators
  "(" "[" "{" | open brackets
  "," "=" ":" ";" | list and binding punctuation
  "|" "<" ">" "!" "&" "-" "~" | operator punctuation ("+" "*" "?" are not openers)
  start of the text being read, or directly after a literal's closing quote | always a token start
TABLE REGISTER — directive NAME | register
  SIR KIND FIDELITY VARIANT ANCHOR SRC TRIGGER REACH ORACLE-CLASS TRACE-SEAM SEAM BEHAVIOR INVARIANT TABLE SCOPE | P
  UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION OUT-OF-DOMAIN ORACLE SHAPE CANONICAL-SHA256 AUTHORITY CARRIED-DATA STATE EMIT REQUEST | P
  BRANCH ON-ERROR PAR RING LIFECYCLE EFFECT SURFACE NOTES GUARD-PINNED, and every RULE NAME | P
  UNIT SIG ENVELOPE DEPENDS-ON DESCRIPTION PINNED EARNED NOTE RATIONALE PROVENANCE CARRIED | B, never a hit
  LANG PRIMARY-SHA256 ABI MECHANISM | L, never a hit
  CITED and any other NAMESHAPE | not a directive here, never a hit
PINNED charset GLUE = the code points that have the Unicode property White_Space or the general category Cf (format), less space, tab, LF and CR, as of Unicode 17.0: "U+000B" "U+000C" "U+0085" "U+00A0" "U+00AD" "U+0600" through "U+0605" "U+061C" "U+06DD" "U+070F" "U+0890" "U+0891" "U+08E2" "U+1680" "U+180E" "U+2000" through "U+200F" "U+2028" through "U+202F" "U+205F" through "U+2064" "U+2066" through "U+206F" "U+3000" "U+FEFF" "U+FFF9" through "U+FFFB" "U+110BD" "U+110CD" "U+13430" through "U+1343F" "U+1BCA0" through "U+1BCA3" "U+1D173" through "U+1D17A" "U+E0001" "U+E0020" through "U+E007F" CITED FIDELITY package — the parser's PINNED glue set, FINDINGS 83; "U+034F", "U+115F", "U+3164" and "U+2800" are NOT in it
INVARIANT every hit's line is the physical line whose text carries it; no line and label appear twice; a language-mode result names each line at most once.
INVARIANT every display holds at most 96 code points and is a prefix of its line less leading spaces and tabs.
ORACLE value -> oracles/lint_leak.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 d774e0dd1d47e7a1cecdd1efdb391598636e71cb451c5247ecc6105b8b8d077c
SCOPE IN: the leak lint over one file's text in either mode, including the per-line reading it applies. OUT: the parser's
  diagnostics (errors and warnings, strict mode), the transcription and pin lints beside it, the family-level lint that
  formats these hits into messages, and how a caller decides is_primary.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (MIT), cli/sir/v05.mjs (DIALECT, stripQuoted, cut96, leakLint) and cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs
  (DIRECTIVES, isDirectiveName, ATTR_ONLY, quotedSpans, glued, INVISIBLE_LEAD, sirLines, splitTrailingComment, splitAttributes, the
  directive reading of parse in legacy mode). Round 5 of REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN Tier 1, re-cut 2026-10-27 after FINDINGS 75, 76
  and 83 were acted on, and delta re-cut 2026-10-27 on 6199e2cc8b75 for FINDINGS 99 and 107 (R1, TABLE OPENERS, R6 c); an independent scan model of R1 to R8 agreed with the package on 900,000 seeded inputs (mulberry32).
